# Publication on screen

A screen publication must remain a readable book before it becomes an interactive
one. Prose, code, links, annotations, and indexes need useful HTML without requiring
client-side JavaScript. Interaction may then add exploration, but it must not be the
only route to a definition.

Generated HTML and the editor preview share semantic rendering. They differ at the
host boundary: generated pages use durable relative links, while the editor grants
short-lived navigation capabilities for cross-document movement.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Model[Shared publication meaning] --> Render[Semantic screen rendering]
    Render --> HTML[Generated HTML page]
    Render --> Preview[Editor preview]
    HTML --> Relative[Relative page links and local anchors]
    Preview --> Capability[Authorized editor destinations]
```

The arrows show that link transport is chosen after semantic rendering has selected
a target. They do not imply that preview reads generated HTML. The diagram omits
styles and optional exploration so the common rendering boundary is easier to see.

## Logical targets survive different transports

A reference first resolves to a fragment family, contribution, annotation, or use.
Only then does the screen adapter ask how to reach that target from the current
document.

Within one page, both screen forms use a local anchor. Across generated pages, the
adapter calculates a relative path, percent-encodes each path component, changes the
chapter suffix to HTML, and appends the stable target. A leading `./` keeps a
filename from being interpreted as a URI scheme.

Across editor previews, the renderer accepts only a destination returned by the
extension's capability adapter. It does not construct editor command URLs or store
source line numbers in a public URL. If resolution is pending or unavailable, the
reference remains text. Logical identity is retained separately from the opaque
transport so a refreshed capability does not redefine the target.

## Merge syntax with meaning

Syntax highlighting and fragment references describe different aspects of the same
code. Highlighting says that a range is a keyword, string, or comment. Semantic
resolution says that a range names another fragment. Neither should discover the
other by inspecting emitted HTML.

The renderer begins with the full displayed contribution. Escaped opening
delimiters become literal text, while actual reference spellings remain intact.
The syntax highlighter returns spans over that display text. The renderer then
intersects those spans with the already known semantic reference ranges.

```mermaid
flowchart TD
    Contribution[Complete contribution text] --> Syntax[Syntax spans]
    Contribution --> References[Semantic reference ranges]
    Syntax --> Merge[Monotonic range merge]
    References --> Merge
    Merge --> Code[Escaped HTML with semantic links]
```

The arrows mean that both inputs address the same text coordinates. Syntax does not
parse fragment names, and references do not infer language tokens. The monotonic
merge visits ordered ranges without repeatedly rescanning the entire contribution.

The screen policy omits comment-only text from displayed program contributions.
A fragment reference inside a comment remains semantic content and therefore stays
navigable even when surrounding comment text is omitted. Unknown languages remain
plain escaped text rather than failing publication.

## Contributions are navigable units

Every accepted contribution receives its own anchor. The first contribution also
carries the family anchor. A declaration label links to that family base, and a
small navigation region can link to the previous, base, and next contributions.

Navigation sits outside the code block. It therefore does not change copied code or
indentation. The region has an accessible label that names the fragment family, and
its controls have descriptive names such as “Next contribution of …”. Preview
anchors retain source mapping for local scroll synchronization.

This presentation keeps the distinction between a family and one of its additions
visible. A family target answers where the definition begins; a contribution target
addresses one authored piece.

## Annotations remain ordinary reading content

Term definitions render as identified sections. Notes render as identified asides.
Their bodies use a closed token vocabulary and preserve paragraphs, lists, emphasis,
strong emphasis, inline code, links, and line breaks. Unsupported leaf content is
escaped; unsupported containers cannot introduce active markup.

A resolved term use becomes a link to the shared term target. A missing or ambiguous
use becomes a span that remains readable and visibly unresolved. Closing tags follow
the same decision as opening tags, preventing malformed markup when resolution
fails.

The title of a term is plain text, not HTML. Parser-owned attributes survive only
where the controlled token requires them, such as a link destination or an ordered
list start. This keeps annotation rendering from becoming a side door for arbitrary
attributes.

## Indexes use model data, not rendered text

When annotations exist, one root page receives a term index and a fragment index.
The term index links to definitions and explicit use occurrences. The fragment
index links to each family, its accepted contributions, and contributions that
reference it.

Reverse-reference maps are built once from the immutable publication snapshot.
Repeated references to the same family within one contribution produce one index
entry for that contribution. This avoids both visual duplication and repeated
whole-project scans.

The implementation caches these indexes weakly by snapshot. A new parse produces a
new snapshot and therefore a new index; closed projects are not retained merely by
the cache.

## Themes are scoped to the publication

A selected catalogue theme supplies both prose and syntax colors. Generated pages
place the stylesheet through the HTML template, while preview uses the host's
integration point. In both cases the rendered book is wrapped in a
`literate-document` element carrying the selected theme identity.

Annotation and index styles are scoped beneath that wrapper. They inherit the
active palette and do not modify unrelated editor or page content. Styling is not
used to communicate identity by color alone: links, headings, borders, labels, and
structural elements retain meaning without the palette.

## Exploration is an optional layer

Ordinary links answer “take me to the definition.” Exploration answers a different
question: “show me how this fragment is composed without leaving my place.” A small
client can add disclosure controls beside semantic reference uses and open nested
panels containing accepted contributions.

```mermaid
stateDiagram-v2
    [*] --> PlainLink
    PlainLink --> Expanded: reader opens exploration
    Expanded --> Expanded: reader opens a nested reference
    Expanded --> PlainLink: reader collapses or content changes
    PlainLink --> [*]
```

The states describe enhancement around an existing link. If the client is absent,
rejected, or removed, the plain link remains. The diagram omits focus details, but
collapse returns focus to a surviving disclosure or baseline anchor when possible.

The payload stores contribution text and reference targets, not ready-made HTML.
The browser creates text nodes and controlled elements. Serialized data escapes
characters that could end a script element. New links are accepted only when they
match the expected target and an allowed local or host capability shape.

## Bounds protect the page

Exploration has limits on payload bytes, nesting depth, panels, parts, segments, and
constructed content. Costs are checked before candidate DOM is allocated. Exceeding
a limit rejects the requested view rather than truncating a fragment into a
misleading partial expansion.

Ownership is private client state, not editable data attributes. Closing a panel
releases the costs of all descendants it owns. A document mutation causes the
client to reconcile controls, retire vanished roots, and remove stale panels.

These limits preserve the baseline publication. The browser client is never allowed
to make the static book unreadable merely because a project is too large to explore
interactively.

## Preview and generated HTML remain peers

Generated HTML embeds the bounded data and a self-contained initializer when usable
references exist. Preview loads the corresponding browser behavior through its
own packaged client and receives destinations from the editor host. Neither form
loads remote code.

The two adapters share contribution rendering, annotation rendering, identity
indexes, syntax merging, and accessibility structure. They do not share authority:
one may construct relative publication URLs, while the other must use editor-granted
capabilities.

Screen publication now has a complete path from semantic model to accessible HTML.
Print begins from the same meaning but must solve different spatial problems:
physical pages, font roles, code breaking, stable references, and annotation rails.
